    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "The em Unit"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The em unit is based on the font size of the parent element."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "html",
      "content": "1<div class=\"parent\">\n2    <p class=\"child1\">This is 2em</p>\n3    <p class=\"child2\">This is 4em</p>\n4</div>"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "css",
      "content": "1.parent {\n2    font-size: 16px;\n3}\n4\n5.child1 {\n6    font-size: 2em;\n7}\n8\n9.child2 {\n10    font-size: 4em;\n11}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Browser Output"
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "em Units",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here,"
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet_list",
      "items": [
        "The parent element has a font size of 16px.",
        "child1 with 2em has a font size of 2 × 16px = 32px.",
        "child2 with 4em has a font size of 4 × 16px = 64px."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So, em units change based on the font size of their parent element, making them useful when you want the text size to adjust relative to other elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
